(Phnom Penh): After the fall of the genocidal regime of Pol Pot on January 7, 1979, the Cambodian government led by the Cambodian People's Party (CPP) has made great efforts to restore the education sector in the worst condition. The semester in Cambodia was reopened on September 24, 1979, just four months after the overthrow of the Khmer Rouge regime.
In the press conference of the Royal Government Spokesperson's Unit on the progress in the field of education on the morning of February 10, 2020, Mr. Dy Kham Poly, Spokesman of the Ministry of Education affirmed that under the high attention of the Royal Government led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen made rapid progress in the education sector 41 years after the country was liberated from the Khmer Rouge regime.
According to Mr. D. Campoli, to date, Cambodia has a total of 14,522 schools across the country, from kindergarten to high school. There are 13,300 public schools. There are 125 higher education institutions, including universities and institutes, of which 48 are state-owned.
Mr. D. Campoli also said that more than 3 million students are studying from kindergarten to higher education, more than 49 percent are female students. There are more than 210,000 students studying in higher education and postgraduate.
At present, there are more than 120,000 educators, teachers and education staff, of which more than 90,000 are teachers.
