Royal Government approves construction of two more coal-fired power plants in Oddar Meanchey and Koh Kong provinces
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government has approved the construction of two more coal-fired power plants in Oddar Meanchey and Koh Kong provinces. Both plants can provide nearly 1,000 megawatts of electricity with an investment of more than 1.6 billion US dollars.
This decision was made at a cabinet meeting chaired by Cambodian Prime Minister Samdech Techo Hun Sen this morning, February 7, 2020. With the approval of the Royal Government, the project to build these two coal-fired plants will be submitted to the National Assembly for review and approval.
Mr. Phay Siphan, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Chairman of the Royal Government Spokesperson Unit, said that a new coal-fired power plant in Oddar Meanchey province will be built in Oddar Meanchey province's Trapeang Prasat district using the existing coal potential. The area is scheduled to open in late 2021 and 2022.
According to the Ministry of Mines and Energy, as of 2019, Cambodia has seven hydropower dams, providing a total of 1,328 megawatts, two coal-fired power plants providing a total of 640 megawatts, and hydropower plants. Black oil consumes a total of 274 megawatts, two solar power plants generate 90 megawatts and biogas generates 56 megawatts.
These power sources provide Cambodia with a total of 2,388 megawatts of electricity in the country. To meet the growing demand for electricity, Cambodia has imported about 442.5 megawatts of electricity from neighboring countries, including Thailand, Vietnam and Laos. Home loan from Chef Bank provides loans up to 70% on home value
