(Phnom Penh): The five-star Hyatt Regis Phnom Penh Hotel, which will open in the third quarter, has made a significant contribution to the preservation of French colonial heritage buildings.
The Hyatt Regency Phnom Penh Hotel, located in the heart of the commercial, cultural and historical district of Daun Penh, preserves a French colonial villa in the center of the hotel, which has been renovated to maintain its solidity but retains its original form and style. Completely.
The preservation of French colonial villas in the center of the hotel aims to preserve the history of the evolution of Cambodian architecture to attract tourists, enhance the value of Cambodian architecture and contribute to the preservation of French colonial buildings.
In addition, the preservation of the French colonial buildings of the Hyatt Regency Phnom Penh Hotel is part of maintaining the identity of the area, as the hotel building has emerged in the commercial area, the National Museum, the Royal Palace, the Royal University of Fine Arts, pagodas and rivers. Chaktomuk with a rich history and culture.
The villa is believed to have been built during the 1900s, possibly belonging to the royal family or high-ranking officials who served in the royal palace, as only royal family or high-ranking officials lived in the area. .
Ministry of Environment Celebrates Wetland Day in Banteay Meanchey to Promote the Great Importance of the Area for Humans and Animals
This year's World Wetlands Day is held under the theme "Wetlands and Biodiversity", which presents to the public the relationship and richness of biodiversity in wetlands, and these are some of the functions of the ecosystem services. Wet soil.
Ken Serey Ratha, president of the Cambodian Wildlife Conservation Association, said the world had lost one-third of its wetlands since 1970. And more than 1 billion people will face water and food shortages. And 40 percent of all wildlife is affected. He said that the Ang Trapeang Thmor Protected Area is being preserved and has become an important tourist destination for research and bird watching today.
He said that through the past bird conservation has been successful, the number of birds has increased steadily. At the same time, there are a number of challenges, such as the challenge of bird sanctuaries. Due to the growth in this area, people are clearing forests, building houses and growing crops that use chemicals.
Separately, Mr. Om Chantha, Deputy Governor of Banteay Meanchey province, said that in Banteay Meanchey province, there are many natural tourism sites and wetlands. He added that the participation in protecting and conserving natural resources for future generations, as well as the development of tourism in the future.
On that occasion, Mr. Srun Darith said that the Royal Government of Cambodia has designated five wetlands as internationally important wetlands, namely the Ramsar site: Stung Treng Ramsar site in Stung Treng province, Koh Kapi Ramsar site in Koh Kong province and The Boeung Chhmar, Prek Toal and Stung Sen Ramsar sites are within the Tonle Sap Lake ecosystem.
In addition, Cambodia has designated a number of important wetlands as protected areas, including five multi-use areas: the Tonle Sap Lake, Dong Peng, Ponhea Krek, Prek Teuk Sap, Kbal Chhay and Boeung Yak Laom. And 6 other landscape protection areas: North lowland landscape protection area, Tonle Sap, Anlong Pring, Ang Trapeang Thmor, Boeung Prek, Boeung Lumpot and Boeung Prek Lpou protection area.
It should be noted that World Wetlands Day is celebrated annually on February 2 to celebrate the ratification of the Convention on Wetlands (Ramsar Convention) on February 2, 1971. For Cambodia, it is one of the 171 signatories in the world to have the obligation to manage, conserve and protect wetlands that are and are of international importance. Ministry of Environment issues Prakas on Environmental Impact Assessment Classification for Development Projects
