(Battambang): To continue the celebration of the 30th anniversary of the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC), all key stakeholders earlier this week met again in a major event at Two Thnou Primary School in Battambang Province.
Cambodia ratified the Convention on the Rights of the Child on 15 October 1992, and since then Cambodia has made significant progress in improving the lives of children. More than 97% of boys and girls enroll in this primary school at an unprecedented rate. Infant and child mortality under the age of five has dropped by more than 70 percent, and access to clean water has tripled since the 1990s.
Not all children enjoy childhood, and most of them are short-lived. Children do not receive the education they deserve, and protection issues remain common, with one in four children reporting some form of violence.
The event highlights the EU's efforts to keep all children safe, provide upbringing and education, and provide unique opportunities to make children's rights a top priority of national and development plans. Not only that, the event also demonstrates the EU's willingness to work closely with rural communities to make these goals a reality.
Mr. Franck VIAULT stated that we are pleased to celebrate the 30th anniversary of the Convention on the Rights of the Child with all of you in Battambang. The convention ensures better implementation of children's rights around the world. It urged the EU and its Member States to change laws, policies and practices aimed at protecting and promoting the rights of the child.
More than 300 children who attended the event also heard from their community members, such as the principal of Pei Thnou Primary School and two children's representatives, Tom Rattanavongboth and Chan Phinu, who shared their stories and showed what children's rights mean. How about them. The event entitled All Rights Reserved for Children is full of activities that bring fun for children and their families.
Ministry of Education Press Release: 23 Cambodian students in Wuhan, China are in good health
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports on February 4, 2020 issued a press release on the current welfare of Cambodian students living in Wuhan, China, where the outbreak of pneumonia caused by the virus. Corona.
According to the Ministry of Education, a total of 89 students in Wuhan, Hubei Province are studying at seven different universities, including 23 students who are currently in Wuhan in good health. And not a single student has contracted this new type of pneumonia due to the overwhelming attention from the leaders of the relevant ministries, institutions at all levels and the student associations there.
The Ministry of Education said that some students receive food from the university every day, three times a day, with the university providing food to the students' dormitories, and some students have to buy their own food from the store at cost. 15 minutes (one purchase can take 3 to 4 days).
The Ministry of Education states that another 66 students returned to Cambodia in early January 2020 in order to visit their hometowns during the winter holidays like every year and get proper leave from each university. ⁇. The university has postponed the vacation and informed students of the resumption date at a later date.
