(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sum Sansith, Deputy Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, on February 4, 2020, requested the local authorities to cooperate in enforcing the law and taking action against some opportunists to deceive the people.
He also urged the relevant partners to educate the public about the correct use of financial services.
Mr. Sum Sannisit made this remark on the occasion of his participation in the dissemination workshop on "Microfinance in Cambodia" in Mondulkiri Province on February 4, 2020.
Mr. Sum Sannith said that in the past, the scam of the crooks has made the debtors in a state of immobility, and some families have lost and destroyed their property. Therefore, local authorities need to work together to enforce the law and take action against some opportunists who use the image to borrow money to deceive people.
He added that in order to increase access to quality financial services and have the control of clear laws and regulations to enable people not to receive or are using informal financial services to turn to financial services. Objects in the system properly inspected by the National Bank of Cambodia.
Mr. Sum Sannisith continued that the microfinance sector in Cambodia plays an important role in contributing to economic and social development, especially the reduction of poverty in rural areas. But even though the sector is now proudly growing, some opportunists have used it as a tool for personal, financial and political gain.
Mr. Sum Sannisith stated that for people who borrow money from banks and legal microfinance institutions must be obliged to repay the institution so that the sector continues to serve the poor to establish business expansion and activities. Period of providing microfinance services in rural areas.
Mr. Rath Sovannarak, Director General of the National Bank of Cambodia, stated that the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has taken care to increase access to affordable and affordable financial services for the general public through Set goals to improve the financial environment, so that people have access to and use of official financial services in a timely manner, appropriate to the needs, affordable and have legal protection.
He went on to say that this financial environment plays an important role in promoting economic growth, reducing inequality and reducing poverty in Cambodia. Currently, about 59% of the adult population in Cambodia has access to formal financial services, of which 17% are provided by banks, 42% are provided by non-bank financial institutions such as microfinance institutions and payment service providers. .
He added that due to the limited financial knowledge of the people, combined with the income and accommodation factors, the adult population who do not receive financial services or are using informal financial services is still high. Up to 41%. This is a concern that the National Bank of Cambodia needs to address through all forms of education, including workshops like this one.
It should be noted that as of the end of 2019, there are 75 non-deposit-taking microfinance institutions, 7 deposit-taking microfinance institutions and 248 rural credit institutions with a network of 1,448 branches nationwide.
