(Phnom Penh): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Interior, said this morning, February 3, 2020, that as of the end of December 2019, local associations and non-governmental organizations (NGOs) have filed documents and A total of 5,564 registered, including 2,238 associations and 3,316 non-governmental organizations.
At the 4th Partnership Forum between the Royal Government and Civil Society Organizations, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng affirmed that after the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations came into force, a total of 966 local associations and non-governmental organizations registered List with the Ministry of Interior in accordance with the law, including 471 associations and 495 non-governmental organizations.
According to Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, in 2019, 38 associations and non-governmental organizations were removed from the list at the request of the relevant associations and non-governmental organizations in accordance with the provisions of the Law on Associations and Non-Governmental Organizations. Non-governmental organizations.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng noted that the number of new local associations and NGOs formed each month has not decreased, with an average of registered local associations and NGOs per month. At the Ministry of Interior, about 20.
In addition, the Ministry of Interior has generally found that the activities of civil society organizations at the grassroots level are usually good, with no restrictions. At the same time, local authorities seem to be more understanding and provide more cooperation with associations and non-governmental organizations and local communities.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng at the Opening Ceremony of the Forum on Partnership between the Royal Government and Civil Society Organizations (4th time) claimed that the dissemination of information by some civil society organizations was a bad intention with the Royal Government, contrary to what It is the commitment of the Royal Government to consider civil society organizations as good partners.
The Minister of Interior expressed his suspicion that if there was a local dispute between the civil society organization and the authorities, the civil society organization would collect the information but did not provide the information to the authorities. This point is like a civil society organization hiding information, so the relationship between the government and civil society organizations is not transparent, as opposed to building a partnership, but a screening.
During the second half of 2019, the Ministry of Interior received only two reports related to inquiries from local authorities, in Battambang province, where commune authorities did not issue certificates of residence and organization of Banteay Meanchey province. The district authority required the organization to submit a request for action, which was contrary to the guidelines of the Ministry of Interior. This is the emphasis of Prince Sar Kheng.
According to Samdech Sar Kheng, in these two cases, the inter-ministerial working group also contacted the two provincial administrations directly to help coordinate and resolve the issues that have arisen. However, the Royal Government of Cambodia remains strongly committed to continuing to promote cooperation in a genuine partnership with civil society organizations in contributing to local economic development.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng also claimed that the Royal Government welcomes what civil society organizations say because the Ministry has a permanent mechanism to solve problems and also has a mechanism for public forums at the national and sub-national levels, but if civil society organizations Hiding information does not know how to solve it.
At the same time, Samdech Sar Kheng requested the civil society organizations and relevant authorities to provide information to each other.
