This season Svay Rieng Cup has 51 teams participating, 107 matches, running for 4 months from January to May 2020, with sports teams from 8 districts throughout Svay Rieng.
All sports teams must have Cambodian players aged 16 and over, a team with a maximum of 23 members and a minimum of 17 members, and each team can bring 3 players from outside the province. And 20 are residents of Svay Rieng province or place of birth in Svay Rieng province.
The formula of the 51 teams is divided into 13 groups, with 4 teams in each group, for the 13th group there are only 3 teams and have to rotate in groups to get the first and second place teams. 2 up to round 32 teams continue.
Separately, the third best team in the group of 6 teams also have a chance to advance to the next round. For the 32nd round, 16 teams, 8 teams, the last 4 teams will be drawn to meet each other.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen attends the "World Summit 2020 of the Global Peace Federation on Peace, Security and Human Development." Samdech Techo Hun Sen Arrives in Seoul Safely (Phnom Penh): At around 1 pm Cambodian time, which is approximately 3 pm in South Korea, on February 3, 2020, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his delegation Senior officials have arrived in Seoul, South Korea.
Arriving at the airport in Seoul, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and his delegation were warmly welcomed by senior South Korean officials and Cambodian Ambassador to South Korea Long Dimanche.
It should be noted that this morning, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, this morning, February 3, 2020, attended the 2020 World Summit of the Global Peace Federation (UPF) on Peace, Security and Human Development. Held in the Republic of Korea (South Korea).
The recent visit of Samdech Techo Hun Sen to South Korea is seen as a new contribution to the promotion of relations and cooperation between Cambodia and South Korea.
Trade volume between Cambodia and South Korea is seen to be growing steadily. The trade volume in 2019 was nearly 1 billion US dollars. Of that, Cambodia's exports to South Korea account for about 30 percent and South Korea imports about 70 percent of Cambodia.
