Cardiologist at Calmette Hospital: To reduce high blood pressure, reduce your intake of salty, high-fat foods, smoking and alcohol.
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Theng Yudalin, a cardiologist at Calmette Hospital, said that in order to reduce high blood pressure (hypertension) for the unborn and the newborn, it is necessary to reduce the intake of salty foods. Fat, smoking and alcohol consumption and return to sports.
In addition, Dr. Theng Yudalin added that these can help reduce the disease and take long-term medication for people with high blood pressure.
The remarks were made at a workshop on "Perspectives on Cardiovascular Care" organized by Allianz Pharmaceutical Cambodia Labosanofi in collaboration with the Cambodian Cardiologists Association at the Phnom Penh Hotel on the 1st of this month. February 2020.
Prof. Dr. Sok Chuor, Head of Cardiology at Calmette Hospital and President of the Cambodian Cardiologists Association, said: “Cambodia's health system has improved significantly in the last decade with increasing budget expenditures. The Royal Government to support the needs of the people of Cambodia and improve the well-being of the people.
He also emphasized that the Cardiovascular Workshop is a refinement of knowledge to be applied in medicine in the treatment and guidance of effective patient health care.
According to Dr. Liu Qing, a cardiologist at Calmette Hospital, people with high blood pressure may be more likely to develop heart disease later in life, which can lead to severe chest pain and lead to obstruction. Cardiovascular disease leads to life-threatening effects.
He added that the Ministry of Health continues to focus on non-communicable diseases, including cardiovascular disease. However, cardiovascular disease still has the highest death toll in Cambodia. High blood pressure and coronary heart disease are the most common cardiovascular diseases and have the highest mortality rates.
This is a concern for Cambodians because we may see an increasing number of older people living with health problems and disabilities caused by cardiovascular disease. Therefore, it is important to stop this problem as soon as possible. This is the additional confirmation of Dr. Liu Qin.
He said a healthy lifestyle and maintaining a healthy weight will reduce the risk of chronic diseases such as high blood pressure, obesity and diabetes, which are major risk factors for cardiovascular disease. These risk factors need to be addressed as soon as possible and are likely to yield the best results.
