(Kandal): Mr. Ros Phon Channimol, Investigating Judge of Kandal Provincial Court, on 31 January 2020, issued a detention order for a police officer after he drove a car and hit a person. The victim caused the injury.
It should be noted that a police officer in Kandal province showed gangster behavior by driving a car and hitting the car of another passenger who was traveling on the road and even beating him without fear or fear of the law at all.
The incident happened on the night of January 25, 2020, recently at Saang Phnom Road in Saang District, Kandal Province, while the cause is not yet clear what the origin of this story. Sure.
He was arrested by Kandal Provincial Police under the command of Brigadier General Chhoeun Socheat, Provincial Police Commissioner, and sent to court until the court issued a detention order.
The police officer was identified as 31-year-old Hen Lina, a resident of Svay Chuor village, Koh Khel commune, Saang district, Kandal province.
Sources at the scene claimed that the victim's car was first seen before the police car. At this time, the police officer backed the car and hit the victim's car and got out of the car and hit the victim angrily, causing the people at the scene to wake up.
According to the source, after the provocation, both parties were summoned to the Saang District Police Inspectorate for questioning, but upon arrival at the police station, the provocative police officers refused to cooperate and even caused violence to relatives. Of more victims.
However, for all these activities, the victims decided to file two complaints at once: the first case of traffic accident and the second case of violence.
Cambodian Microfinance Association: Three Challenges May Slow Growth in the Microfinance Sector by 2020
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, on January 31, 2020, highlighted that the three challenges include the outbreak of coronary heart disease, EBA and the decline of the Cambodian economy (GDP). At 6 percent, the microfinance sector is expected to slow growth in 2020.
This emphasis was made on the occasion of Mr. Kea Boran's participation in the 15th General Assembly and the 1st Annual Forum between the Regulators and the members of the Cambodian Microfinance Association on Improving the Operating Environment of the Microfinance Sector. Cambodian objects held at Phnom Penh Hotel, Phnom Penh this morning, January 31, 2020.
Speaking to reporters, Kea Boran said: "Normally, if Cambodia's GDP growth slows down, I think it will have some impact because we lend money to the economy, so if the economy "We have a decrease in demand for capital, it also decreases. If it decreases, we can not release capital."
However, Mr. Kea Boran also said to wait and see the latest developments in the short term, how far this evolution has evolved?
"I think we will have to wait and see what developments happen in the near future, but these three factors can be a challenge for the banking sector as a whole," he said. "But the microfinance sector is subject to change, so we expect that in 2020 our growth may not be as high as in 2019 due to these three factors."
Vongsey Visoth, Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of Economy and Finance, on January 30, 2020, affirmed that Cambodia's economic growth in 2020 will still be around 6%, despite the difficulties and risks, both internal and external. More externally, especially in the case of the Coronavirus outbreak in China, Cambodia's largest economic partner.
He said that in the 2020 budget law, the Royal Government of Cambodia has projected that Cambodia's economy will reach 6.5 percent, including the withdrawal of the EBA from the European Union, because Cambodia knows that the EBA withdrawal will Arrive just before or after. He said that even if the EU removes the preferential trade system in whole or in part, the Cambodian economy will not collapse because Cambodia is already prepared.
