Cambodian Microfinance Association: The Cambodian microfinance sector has provided more than $ 7 billion in loans to about 2 million clients.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Kea Boran, Chairman of the Board of Directors of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, said on January 31, 2020 that the Cambodian microfinance sector has provided more than 7 billion US dollars in loans to about 2 million customers. .
This statement was made on the occasion of Mr. Kea Boran's participation in the 15th General Assembly and the 1st Annual Forum between the Regulators and the members of the Cambodian Microfinance Association on Improving the Operating Environment of the Microfinance Sector. Cambodian Finance held at Phnom Penh Hotel, Phnom Penh this morning, January 31, 2020.
Responding to a reporter's question "How many million clients has he borrowed from microfinance institutions across Cambodia and what is the loan size?" Kea Boran replied, "The number of borrowers has increased by about 2.1 million, not too many borrowers, and about $ 7 billion in loans, which has seen a significant increase in the number of loans."
According to a press release issued by the Cambodian Microfinance Association published today, "As of September 2019, the Cambodian microfinance sector has provided about $ 7 billion in loans to about 2 million customers, while the number of savers "Nearly 3 million people, with nearly 4 billion in 1,400 offices with 34,000 employees."
The Cambodian Microfinance Association added that the financial sector has changed significantly in the last 10 years. Initially, the loan portfolio increased 18 times, while the number of customers doubled. In addition, the sector has changed dramatically from a development organization to a privately owned financial institution.
The sector has focused heavily on small and medium-sized enterprises, while a small number of banks have also jumped into the market. The state interest rate cap in April 2017 kept the number of customers stable, but the average loan size increased significantly. This growth has led to praise as well as concerns that there may be challenges such as debt consolidation due to the intense competition in the sector.
Mr. Kea Boran said, "Responsible financial environment projects respond more to concerns and contribute to economic growth, which gives Cambodians the right to access more formal financial services while Samak has been working on this with its members since 2017.
The Chairman of the Board of the Microfinance Association of Cambodia stated that the project is expected to:
Please be informed that the Cambodian Microfinance Association (MFI) today inaugurated the Responsible Financial Environment Project with the goal of responding to concerns and contributing to economic growth by making Cambodians more accessible. Responsible official financial services.
This three-year project, funded by members of the Cambodian Microfinance Association, will contribute to the National Bank of Cambodia's National Strategy on the Financial Environment 2019-2025.
The Cambodian Microfinance Association is the only institution representing all legally registered microfinance institutions to implement projects worth nearly $ 1 million in close collaboration with its members and key partners such as the National Bank of Cambodia. Of Cambodia, National Bank of Cambodia Association, Cambodia Credit Bureau and development partners.
