Samdech Pichey Sena Tea Banh Meets the Minister of Defense of the Republic of Turkey
Accompanying Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh include: General Ith Sarath, Deputy Commander and Joint Chiefs of Staff of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, General Nem Sovath, Special Adviser and Director General of the General Department of Politics and Foreign Affairs, Ministry of National Defense and General Chao Phirun, Director General of the General Department of Technical Equipment, Ministry of National Defense.
On that occasion, Mr. Hulusi AKAR, Minister of Defense of Turkey, welcomed the visit of the delegation of the Ministry of Defense of Cambodia to the Republic of Turkey. He stressed that although the two countries, Cambodia and Turkey are far apart, but we can cooperate with each other in many fields between the two countries.
The Turkish Defense Minister considered the visit of Prince Tea Banh to the Republic of Turkey very important because it provided a good opportunity for the two armies to share knowledge and experience with each other.
The minister also mentioned the training of the army through exchange programs in the military academy, as well as the exchange of military personnel in other fields. "We can share our experiences and knowledge in the field of defense and work together for the benefit of both countries as well as for the cause of peace and stability in the region," he said.
In response, Samdech Pichey Sena Tea Banh confirmed that this is the first visit of the Cambodian National Defense Delegation to Turkey and also the implementation of the principles of the Head of the Royal Government provided to strengthen and organize relations. Military cooperation between the two armies.
Samdech Pichey Sena Tea Banh expressed his belief that although there are not many areas of cooperation yet, we have looked at a number of tasks, including increasing technical cooperation, industry and military equipment, some of which have been in the past. Come on.
Exchanging views with the sharing of information, terrorism and policy, mutual experience through political defense skills, and being able to consult and provide experience in the military sector with guarantees and social principles, as well as a number of other issues.
