"Under most countries' intellectual property laws, there are provisions on the protection of collective trademarks," he said.
"Collective brand" is generally defined as a mark that identifies the origin, geography, raw material, production method, or other general characteristics of the goods or services of the enterprises that use the collective brand.
He added that collective trademark owners can be associations with member enterprises or other entities, including public institutions or cooperatives, and registered trademark owners are responsible for ensuring compliance with certain standards. Any (usually defined in the Common Trademark Usage Regulations) by its members. Therefore, the function of the collective brand is to inform the public about the characteristics of the products that carry the collective brand.
He added that collective brands are often used to promote products with unique features offered by a particular region. In such cases, the establishment of a collective brand not only helps to market local and foreign products, but also provides a framework for cooperation between local producers.
In fact, the creation of a collective brand must go hand in hand with the development of common standards, criteria and strategies. He stressed that Mark Soapheap can be a powerful tool for local development by working together to enable producers, traders and small and medium enterprises to benefit from the economy and broaden brand recognition by shifting from Production goes to market in the same way as other large companies.
A collective trademark is a visual mark used to apply for registration and can identify the origin of goods or services, or identify other common characteristics, including the quality of goods or services of different enterprises that use the mark. That is under the control of the registered collective trademark owner.
