China is the world's second largest economy after the United States. Thus, any forecast of a gross domestic product (GDP) growth rate in China's economy is linked to the impact of the global economic downturn. The outbreak of the new coronavirus has already caused countries and economists to become increasingly anxious and concerned about the inevitable international impact.
Chinese New Year is the most important holiday and also the time when the world's largest Chinese people travel the most. Reports say more than 7 million people are planning to travel abroad. But actual travel is estimated to have dropped by 40 percent compared to 2019.
Large-scale shipping closures in China have severely affected the industry and affected the export-import sector as well. China is now the world's manufacturing hub, and protracted pressures on shipping will affect parts of the world.
The full impact of the coronavirus on China and the world economy will be known after the spread of the virus is resolved and businesses resume normal operations. But initial estimates say an outbreak of the coronavirus could slow China's economic growth by 0.5 to 1 percent. At 1 percent, China's economic losses will be more than $ 136 billion, which is huge.
According to the Asian Development Bank (ADB), the SARS outbreak has caused $ 18 billion in economic losses. But the global impact caused by the new coronavirus could be even greater, as the human impact has already been shown, with 35 million people stranded and threatened by the virus. This.
