Residents transporting live chickens from Thailand through O'Smach, Oddar Meanchey province, request S2V to reduce transportation costs
According to a representative of the chicken transport business, in the past, transporting live chickens from Thailand through the O'Smach channel, which has about 18 live chickens weighing about 20 kilograms, costs only 8,400 riel. However, since S2V received the exclusive right to pay up to 1,800 riel per basket, the people who are transporting live chickens erupted in protest, reducing the price to 18,000 riel per basket.
According to the live chicken transport business, in addition, the company has limited the delivery of chickens, and for transporters who do not have a receipt from the company, the authorities will arrest immediately.
In addition, traders transporting chickens for resale in Siem Reap and nearby provinces have to pay the company 500 riel per kilogram of live chickens, but if transporting to Phnom Penh, they have to pay the company. One kilogram up to 1,000 riel.
On the other hand, according to the traders, S2V currently has two trucks to transport chickens to Phnom Penh by itself, which makes people who want to buy a car to transport a taxi risk losing their jobs without money. The bank. In general, the company was immediately shut down, with a chicken for 2,500 riel delivered to Phnom Penh.
In this case, a group of people transporting chickens from Thailand and traders buying chickens for resale in the provinces requested the relevant professional institutions and provincial authorities to help monitor the activities of S2V, because today Doing whatever you want. S2V representatives could not be reached for comment.
(Phnom Penh): Chip Mong Industries is open to students who are studying at various universities to visit the production chain, technology and factory management throughout the year.
Students can learn about the production line, technology and factory management through the presentations of Chip Mong Industries' professional engineers and visit the production techniques of modern machines in the factory, the selection of raw materials and the production line. In the factory of ready-mixed concrete, pipes and concrete tiles, Chip Mong to supply the construction sector.
University students wishing to learn more about production, technology and factory management can contact the company directly for more information on arranging monthly factory visits.
Chip Mong Industries regularly receives study visits from students from various universities such as Paragon International University, Institute of Technology of Cambodia, Norton University and other universities. Ministry of Labor Announces Code of Conduct for Cambodian Private Recruitment Agencies, Ensuring Strengthening Workers' Rights
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Labor and Vocational Training has announced the introduction of a code of conduct for Cambodian private recruitment agencies, believing that it will ensure the successful migration of workers, successful outcomes and positive experiences, as well as the protection of their rights. They too.
The promulgation of the Code of Conduct for the Cambodian Private Recruitment Agency was presided over by Mr. Ith Sam Heng on the morning of January 29, 2020 at the Phnom Penh Hotel.
This Code is a tool that can be implemented by all Cambodian private recruitment agencies, setting out the basic principles in accordance with national law and international labor standards for all Cambodian private recruitment agencies to implement effectively and responsibly. And respect high ethics in the management, recruitment, training, transfer and management of workers to work abroad.
This code will contribute to strengthening the effectiveness of the protection and promotion of labor rights as well as the well-being of workers both at home and abroad. In addition, it will play an important role in supporting the performance of the Cambodian Recruitment Agency in a responsible manner and is also a useful resource for migrant workers to confirm the expectations of workers from the relevant private recruitment agency. And their work migration experience.
Mr. Pablo Kang, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, said that the introduction of a code of conduct for Cambodian private recruitment agencies at this time was important and that Australia, along with ASEAN member countries, was working to eliminate migrant workers. Excessive fees, replacement of forced labor contracts and human trafficking.
Minister Ith Sam Heng said that the Ministry of Labor has continued to pay attention to workers working abroad and expand the overseas market by continuing to provide five services to Cambodian workers abroad:
First, implement the ASEAN Declaration on the Protection and Promotion of the Rights of Migrant Workers. Has been working to help workers working abroad to ensure legal benefits. Help provide legitimacy to illegal workers. Assign employment advisers at embassies of countries with workers such as Thailand, Korea, Malaysia and Japan.
Second, cooperate with partner countries in recognizing each other's skills, such as Cambodia and Thailand have recognized construction skills such as masonry and electrical wiring. In the Republic of Korea, workers are given the opportunity to go to vocational training schools and receive certification. After returning to Cambodia, you can have clear skills.
Third, cooperating with ASEAN countries in the field of social security
Fourth, it is cooperating with ASEAN countries to implement the pension system so that workers can transfer their membership, seniority and social security contributions from one country to another after the end of the employment contract and to be the basis for charging the pension. .
Fifth, facilitated the procedure for foreign workers to transfer the right to the family in the country as a representative to apply for a letter of condemnation or an administrative letter or a certificate of identity card without the person having to return home.
Currently, Cambodia has sent workers to work abroad in six countries: Thailand, the Republic of Korea, Malaysia, Japan, Singapore and Hong Kong, with a total of about 1.2 million workers.
