To repair and reduce this risk, Ms. Pich Jira stated that the experts first repaired the north side, which is very dangerous, especially the location of torn bricks up to 21 cm in the north wall. She added that experts then repaired the internal and external cracks to prevent water seepage and prevent any further damage.
As for the technology, she said that the damaged site, the team dismantled the decayed bricks and repaired parts, maintaining the original form of the temple and preserving the ancient techniques and preserving the historical value of the temple architecture.
On the other hand, the team searched for old and reusable bricks, including adding new ones in accordance with the level of damage to the building and adhering to the theory and preservation of cultural heritage (Angkor Charter and ICC-Angkor). For traditional mixing techniques, skilled workers with many years of experience in repairing brick temples will also be involved.
Free trade talks between Cambodia and China start with good results
