New Australian ambassador vows to boost investment and trade cooperation with Cambodia
On the morning of the 28th, Mr. Pabio Chihukang made a statement on the strengthening of investment and trade cooperation between Cambodia and Australia during his courtesy call and discussion with Samdech Techo Hun Sen. January 2020 at the Peace Palace.
During the meeting, Ambassador Pabio Chihukang said that coming to Cambodia as an Ambassador is a pleasant mission, especially to a country with good relations and cooperation with Australia. This was confirmed by Mr. Eang Sophalleth, Samdech Techo Hun Sen's personal assistant, to the media after the meeting.
Mr. Eang Sophalleth emphasized that in this meeting, the Australian Ambassador expressed his commitment to promote and enhance cooperation in various fields with the Ministry of Institutions of Cambodia, especially on investment and trade between Both countries.
The new Australian Ambassador said that Australia is currently diverting cooperation directly to Cambodia's ministries and institutions. The Australian Government is currently working with the Ministry of Finance on Taxation, the Ministry of Mines and Energy, the Ministry of Public Works, the Ministry of Health, the Ministry of Interior, the Ministry of Agriculture and the Ministry of Defense.
Mr. Pabio Chihukang stated that during his current diplomatic mission to Cambodia, he will continue to promote cooperation with all institutions with Cambodia and also focus on cooperation, especially on trade. He added that Australia is open to Cambodians to visit, invest and study there. In this regard, he will pay special attention to investment and trade issues, as well as cooperation with Cambodia.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen welcomed and congratulated the Ambassador on his appointment in Cambodia. The Prime Minister agreed with the Ambassador's remarks on the Australian Government's direct cooperation with Cambodian ministries and institutions.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen said that Cambodia and Australia have many relations with institutions and institutions, and also have special relations within the framework of the United Nations, ASEAN, East Asia, as well as cooperation partners in all fields. Also.
In addition, Australia also has bilateral cooperation, especially Australia has helped Cambodia in the search for peace in the past. In particular, Australia has previously made it a priority to assist Cambodia in agriculture, and in the field of agriculture, Australia has established the Cardi Institute, which has helped promote Cambodian rice yields and help increase rice yields. Cambodia has also become the number one rice in the world.
Samdech Techo also agreed and expressed his pleasure at the attention paid to the cooperation on trade by the Australian Ambassador. In this regard, Samdech Techo Hun Sen expressed the view that strong political relations come from trade relations, so Samdech supports the strengthening of cooperation on investment and trade.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng: Reforming the Identity Management System Remains a High Priority for the Royal Government
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of the Ministry of Interior, said that it is necessary for each country to prepare for the integration into the digital economy in order to keep up with the neighboring countries in the region in national development and rapid development. Modern technology, therefore, the reform of the identity management system remains a high priority for the Royal Government of the sixth mandate of the National Assembly.
In the 4th Meeting of the National Steering Committee on Civil Status, Civil Statistics and Identification (NCDD) to Review the Draft Law on Civil Statistics, Statistics and Identification on the morning of January 28, 2020 Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng claims that the management system of this identification system is the backbone of all government service delivery systems, both public and private.
Prince Sar Kheng noted that many countries around the world are now focusing on expanding control over the identity of their people and improving it through modernization of management systems. And the transformation of manual registration systems, such as registrars, residency management, identity cards, and passports, into information-based registration systems.
Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng continued that after reviewing the report on the evaluation of the implementation of the OWSO, the identification services in Kampot and Kep provinces and the progress report on the preparation of the draft law on civil status, statistics, census and identification. The draft law is the first major step in paving the way for a system for registering and managing personal identities in line with global standards.
