In a press conference on "Work progress of the Ministry of Environment and updates on air quality in Cambodia" organized by the Spokesperson of the Royal Government on the morning of January 27, 2020, Mr. Heng Nareth confirmed that the plan to purchase monitoring equipment. Particles in the air to be used in Svay Rieng and Banteay Meanchey provinces, which are not yet equipped and added in some major cities.
Heng Nareth claims that the device will be ordered from China for about $ 5,000 each.
Last week, air quality in Cambodia, especially in Phnom Penh, was found to have increased particulate matter in the air, leading to concerns about the impact on people's health. Mr. Heng Nareth emphasized that the Ministry of Environment and the General Department of Environmental Protection regularly monitor this issue with 31 air quality monitoring equipment installed in Phnom Penh and in the provinces.
Mr. Heng Nareth said that currently the air quality monitoring equipment is available in Phnom Penh 7 stations, Siem Reap 2 stations, Sihanoukville 2 stations and in other cities in each province.
The Director General of the General Department of Environmental Protection claims that through these air quality monitors, data is provided on air quality assessments every half hour. The devices also found that last week's increase in air pollution was due to an increase in the particle concentrations of PM2.5 and PM10, while the source of the gas pollution did not increase. .
However, Heng Nareth said that until this week, air quality had returned to normal and there were no problems to worry about.
Regarding the issue of air quality, the Director General called on all people to follow the information presented by the Ministry of Environment.
In this press conference, Mr. Heng Nareth also confirmed that the problem that led to the decline in air quality. Problems leading to declining air quality include emissions and emissions from diesel-powered vehicles and other fuels, such as burning black oil and coal.
Wildfires, burning of forest land for ownership, burning of fields, such as burning rubbish and rice husks, are also sources of declining air quality.
Mr. Heng Nareth called on people to participate in maintaining air quality without burning garbage in the open.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said in the press conference of the spokesperson of the Royal Government of Cambodia on the progress of the Ministry of Environment and the current state of air quality in Cambodia, the Office of the Council of Ministers on January 27, 2020 that the total amount of waste generated in Cambodia is more than 10,000 tons per day and a total of nearly 4 million tons of waste per year, and the growth rate of waste in Cambodia is about 15% per year.
"Therefore, in order to ensure effective waste management, avoidance of pollution and public health, it is necessary to pay more attention to the construction of the field in accordance with the technical characteristics and the amount of waste," he said. Established daily and need to prepare land for expansion of additional pitches according to actual needs. "Most landfills are small and can hold small amounts of waste."
