(Kampong Chhnang): People living along the river in Chroy Sleng village, Trapeang Chan commune, Bariboun district, they are engaged in the business of selling oysters. Complied with the law on fisheries in oyster fishing, which does not affect the biodiversity of the river.
Traditional fishing or hand fishing to support the livelihood of the above fishermen is not prohibited by law.
This type of oyster fishing has been around since ancient times, so it does not affect the river bottom biodiversity, as they use the force of the oysters to push the oysters into the pockets by hand, unable to penetrate deep into the riverbed by hand and not as a tool. Especially for machine-powered glues, which are banned as traders operate every day.
Because of these devices, it seriously affects the bottom biodiversity and leads to the extinction of some fish that spawn and live in the mud of the river.
In fact, on January 22, 2020, in Chroy Sleng village, Trapeang Chan commune, Baribo district, it was observed that nearly 100 fishing boats of Chroy Sleng villagers were floating on the surface of Tonle Sap Lake, and some of them drifted from In the water and some are picking oysters on the boat in the middle of the day. In each fishing boat, there are 3 to 4 sprockets of oysters.
Len Channy, who was sitting on a smash boat, said that during the dry season, she and her husband, as well as almost all the villagers, came to the lake by hand every year because of the hand-washing. As such, the fisheries authorities do not ban them, and they clearly understand the destruction of the river bottom biodiversity, so they fish this oyster in the traditional way.
