(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan, through its Embassy in Cambodia, has provided a total grant of $ 175,308 to two units in Kampong Cham and Tbong Khmum provinces under the Grant Assistance Program for Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusano Chea ).
The signing ceremony was held on the afternoon of January 22, 2020, signed by Mr. Mikami Masahiro, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia and representatives of both Recipients for the following projects: To:
First: Koh Svay Irrigation Rehabilitation Project, Kampong Cham Province, budget $ 89,508 Recipient Unit: Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, Kampong Cham Province.
Second: Project to strengthen agricultural communities in Memot and Tambor districts, Tbong Khmum province, budget $ 85,800, Recipient unit: Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries, Tbong Khmum province.
In this project, a warehouse will be built and a pepper mill and cashew processing equipment will be installed in two farming communities, one in Memot district and the other in Tbong Khmum province.
Both farming communities have struggled to increase the value of their commercial crops, such as pepper and cashew, due to a lack of suitable processing and storage facilities. It is expected that about 450 farmers who are members of the farming community will benefit from improving the quality of their agricultural products and deepening the value chain in the farming community's business.
Japan's "Kusanoni" grant began in Cambodia in 1991 to support Cambodia's efforts to develop and rebuild the country at the grassroots level. This assistance aims to protect vulnerable people from a number of factors, such as poverty and misfortune, which can directly threaten their livelihoods, livelihoods and dignity, as well as promote community self-reliance. .
Since 1991, the Government of Japan has provided more than $ 60 million to local authorities and NGOs to implement 620 "Kusanoni" projects throughout Cambodia.
