(Phnom Penh): In order to reduce traffic accidents in Cambodia, the Road Safety Committee continued to promote road safety before the Chinese New Year along National Road 6 and distributed helmets free of charge.
Ms. Men Manvi, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Public Works and Transport and Secretary General of the Road Traffic Safety Committee, said on January 22, 2020 that today, the National Road Safety Committee is promoting traffic safety. Recent summits and to reduce traffic accidents are now alarmingly high.
She added that the traffic safety promotion also coincided with the Chinese New Year, which wakes people up to be careful when visiting tourist destinations.
She also said that the distribution of helmets also has explanations and advice from drivers to make them understand the traffic to be safe. She also urged people to take care of their own lives, to obey the law while driving.
"We have traffic rules, so we have to obey the law, do not drive too fast, drive carefully, respect the right of way before turning, always look left and right and be right," she said.
The Secretary of State also said that during the upcoming Chinese New Year, some people are really drinking alcohol, so if you drink alcohol, do not drive, if you drive, please rest and get drunk before driving.
At the same time, she also called on all drivers of cars and motorcycles to thoroughly inspect their vehicles before leaving to avoid any accident and harm to others.
She also said that the Road Safety Committee will promote traffic safety on some national roads and in the provinces. However, she also claimed that some people have not obeyed the traffic law. The King gave a letter of commendation to 16 staff members of the Documentation Center of Cambodia.
(Phnom Penh): His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, on January 22, 2020, presented a letter of commendation to 16 staff members of the Documentation Center of Cambodia who sacrificed their physical and mental strength to serve the cause of Educate about genocide.
In response to the above letter, Mr. Youk Chhang, Director of the Documentation Center of Cambodia, would like to thank His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni for his continued attention and support to the staff and work of the Documentation Center. Cambodia forever.
The purpose of this letter is to congratulate and commend the staff who have worked "for more than 10 years at the Documentation Center of Cambodia".
"The staff of the Center has played an important role in ensuring that the work of the Center of Cambodia can achieve its long-term mission of promoting a fair memory," the King said in a royal letter. And reconciliation. ”
It should be recalled that the King was also a victim of him during the Khmer Rouge regime. According to the coffin of King Norodom Sihanouk in the documentary "Don't Forget Me" he lost three daughters, two sons, two sons (son-in-law) and a son-in-law ) 14 who died during the Khmer Rouge regime.
Cambodia has about 5 million survivors of the Khmer Rouge out of a population of 16 million. DC-Cam was established in 1995 as one of the offices of the Cambodian Genocide Program at Yale University in the United States. Under the leadership of Youk Chhang, a survivor of the Khmer Rouge regime, the center became an independent research institution in 1997.
