(Phnom Penh): While natural resources play an important role in maintaining the ecosystem of the planet, people in the world today, such as in Europe and the United States, have chosen not to use furniture made of natural wood, but support the use Furniture made from raw materials from plantations, which experts believe reduces the pressure on the use of natural wood and preserves the forest's natural resources.
According to data from the Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone, tens of thousands of cubic meters of processed wood have been produced and exported abroad, as local raw materials for processing wood are not yet sufficient to meet demand. Wood, plywood, plywood, wood and furniture, kitchen equipment, cabinets and other materials are imported from abroad, depending on customer orders.
Steven Zhao, another general manager of the wood processing company, said his company had bought acacia wood from local growers for processing.
Liu Xianglin, director of the Plywood and Cowwood Wood Factory in Sihanoukville Special Economic Zone, said Cambodian-made products were exported to the United States and the European Union. Mr. Liu Xianglin welcomed the project to plant wood for processing, saying that if there is more domestic wood processing, it will help reduce the import of processed wood from abroad, and his company is ready to buy for processing.
"The development of plantations is valuable in supplying wood raw materials for the wood processing industry, while construction in Cambodia has been growing and growing, especially in Phnom Penh and the provinces," said Luo Chouchang. Sihanoukville, where many new building projects are booming.
Mr. Lu Chuchang emphasized that since November 2019, Sing Bay Otec has started clearing 5,000 hectares and plans to start planting seedlings during May 2020 on all the cleared land. With plans to plant nearly 7 million trees.
"The plantations provide a lot of jobs for about 100,000 people in the process of planting and harvesting and processing," said Luo Chouchang. "Like in Vietnam, where they plant plantations, they export to Europe every year, earn billions of dollars and create a lot of jobs for the people."
He highlighted the company's efforts to develop plantations to serve the wood processing industry in Cambodia and discussions with people and communities living in the area who have vacant land to co-plant seedlings as partners. Jobs for people and improve their lives. He also announced the search for other partners to invest in plantations.
The global timber market is estimated at $ 200 billion.
Regarding the investment in plantations in Cambodia, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, expressed support and said that investment in plantations is really important because it will reduce the pressure. The use of natural wood and the establishment of a wood processing industry will also help create jobs and increase income for people in the area to participate in national economic growth.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra stated that the establishment of plantations is an effective strategy to prevent natural resource crime and contribute to strengthening the conservation of natural resources that Cambodia currently has and can sell the role of forests in the framework of carbon credit.
He stressed that Cambodia now has rich natural resources and biodiversity. Forests cover about 50% of the country, the highest rate in Southeast Asia.
