Authorities suspend construction sites and warehouses built illegally in Kambol district
(Phnom Penh): Construction sites and warehouses built illegally in Khan Kampol, Phnom Penh, were suspended by the authorities this morning, January 20, 2020, located in Snor West Village, Snor Sangkat. .
The operation was led by Mr. Kun Chan Sopheary, Deputy Director of Khan Administration and Mr. Van Dollar, Director of Khan Planning and Development Office under the direction of Mr. Khim Sun Soda, Governor of Kambol District, after finding that the construction exceeded the legal request and suspected to be in violation of technical standards. Even more so.
Upon arrival at the site, the authorities inspected the permit and measured the size of the warehouse and the fence surrounding the warehouse and found that it exceeded the construction request and construction permit.
Mr. Kun Chan Sopheary, Deputy Director of District Administration, said that the owner of this construction site was Sok Chanthou, female, and before the construction, he asked for permission from the district authority, but after getting permission, the construction was exceeded. The law instead.
According to the actual measurement, the size of the warehouse under construction in the law only asked for 300 square meters, but actually built up to 900 square meters, while the fence proposed only 110 meters long, but actually built up to 218 meters.
The Deputy Director of Administration continued that after finding the offense that could cause risk at any time, the district governor ordered a temporary suspension of construction, waiting for the owner to apply for the law properly. Allow construction to continue. Not only that, when the authorities will go to inspect the quality of construction for the second time again with professional officers of the District Land Office to prevent any risk of any eventuality in the future.
According to the report, out of the 10 students who continued to faint, there were 6 old patients who fainted the day before and 4 new ones, and today, the total number of students who fainted increased to 52. .
