(Kampong Chhnang): The crime of using illegal machines to sell glue in Kampong Chhnang province to sell in Vietnam is happening every day and also criticized every day, while some officials under the Kampong Chhnang Fisheries Administration try to hide the activities of glue. This is just a family, it is very difficult to suppress them.
In fact, on the evening of January 19, 2020, the media spotted the activities of transporting more than 30 oyster boats with more than 700 bags of oysters were being lifted ashore at the mouth of the port in Prek Sala village, Teuk Hot commune, Rolea Bier district. Sold to traders by truck to sell in Vietnam.
The illegal glazers told reporters that they were glued from Prey Kry village, Peam Chkork commune, Choul Kiri district and sold to traders at the mouth of the port in Prek Sala village. They are shipped to Vietnam. As for shellfish weighing 70-80 kg per bag, they sell for 13,000 riel.
In relation to the above crimes, the journalists immediately reported to Mr. Ngin Hun, Director of the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries. Mr. Ngin Hun then ordered Mr. Ly La, Director of Fisheries District, to arrest the perpetrators of the fishing offenses together with the above exhibits. But within minutes, it exploded and all the sailors boarded a boat to escape, leaving more than 700 bags of oysters at the mouth of the canal in Prek Sala village.
Not only that, sources from the authorities and the people also said that every day, illegal migrants in Chhnuk Trou commune are gliding, breaking fishing rods and destroying roads every day, almost everywhere due to transportation. Transported by these large trucks.
As for the glue people, they stole mobile shells every day at the ports of Yukunt, Chhnuk Trou, Sesab and Kampong Ou, which were transported by trucks. Above.
Everyone urged Mr. Veng Sakhon, Minister of Agriculture, to take action to prevent the case of the above-mentioned shellfish transport to preserve fishery resources as well as shellfish in the river bottom, which is the fish feed and biodiversity of the nation to survive.
