Khmer ox carts are currently working in the tourism sector
(Kampong Chhnang): If we talk about the Khmer ox cart, every Cambodian, young and old, really knows its symbol clearly, because the ox cart has been present in our wonderful land since pre-Angkorian as well as Angkor. At that time, the ox cart played the most important role in Cambodian society, not only in the field of social affairs, but also in the field of religious beliefs.
From the beginning of the first century to the present day, Khmer ox carts are still popular for the Cambodian people in transporting various agricultural products. In fact, people in three villages in Kampong Tralach commune have been using Khmer ox carts to transport tourists to earn some money to support their families.
In addition to rice farming, dryland farming and fishing, people have been in this business for almost 19 years, earning from $ 10 to $ 15 per day, depending on the number of visitors. But now they have formed a community in those three villages.
Mr. Nou Phalkun, Deputy Director of the Department of Tourism of Kampong Chhnang Province, said that in Kampong Tralach district, the most popular resort for foreign tourists to visit is Wat Kampong Tralach Leu or Wat Por Rukkaram, because this pagoda was built in 1672 and a temple was built. Built with laterite bricks and paintings on the walls of the temple since 1847, and also want to know about the Khmer ox cart of our nation.
The number of tourists in each year has been increasing steadily, and the most visitors are in November and December, the income of transporting guests of the people of the three villages above, some also put into savings in the community. Also to hire teachers to teach English to citizens who drive ox carts and other expenses.
He said that in the future, the department plans to prepare traditional Khmer clothes, shirts and jackets for the people in the community so that tourists can get to know the traditional Khmer clothes and arrange for the people to display souvenirs that are works. Khmer sells to customers at reasonable prices. Rainstorms threaten eastern Australian states in addition to wildfires (Photo inside)
