A baby elephant died in O'Lao on the border between Ratanakkiri and Mondulkiri provinces
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment said: "According to the autopsy, the elephant did not have any significant scars that could be presumed to have died for any other reason. What was noticeable was that the hind leg of the baby elephant was broken. "Which is suspected to be crushed by rocks because O'Lao has deep canyons and many rocks."
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that it is very sad to lose this baby elephant. He added that due to the difficult transportation situation, the team found it difficult to save the baby elephant. The team decided to drag the carcass of the baby elephant and bury it in the ground near the mouth of the stream to allow the meat to decompose before collecting the bones to be used as a statue later.
Regarding the number of elephants in Cambodia, Mr. Net Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment, said that currently there are more than 500 wild elephants living in Cambodia, according to a study of wild elephants in Cambodia. There are more than 110 animals in the Keo Seima Wildlife Sanctuary in the Cardamom Mountains, about 200 of which live in Cambodia's protected areas.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment also said that according to the study, the number of wild elephants in Cambodia, as well as in other countries in Southeast Asia, has declined significantly in recent decades due to poaching. Laws for the destruction of elephant habitats and conflicts between elephants and humans, while some people's farms are planted next to the forest and sometimes elephants come to eat crops.
Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment Neth Pheaktra said that the number of domestic elephants raised by locals is about 70. The number of domestic elephants is actually declining due to lack of natural reproduction, and some elephants are aging and subsequently dying.
At the same time, the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment added that in measures to prevent illegal wildlife trade, especially the exchange of ivory, rhinoceros, etc., the Ministry of Environment has already warned by issuing letters to business owners selling about 100 souvenirs. In Phnom Penh, Siem Reap and Sihanoukville, stop storing or selling these prohibited goods and hand them over to the authorities, if they continue to do so, they will be punished according to the law.
