In his annual address to the National Assembly, Vladimir Putin made the following proposals:
Powerful ambitions are anticipated by Putin's proposal to retain the "State Council," which he has established since 2000. The Council of State is expected to gain more power after the amendment of the Constitution, and Putin may form a new top office or chair the council when his term ends in 2024. .
On the other hand, if the constitution is amended, the presidency will no longer be as powerful as it used to be, so he will not have to run for office again.
"Instead of using the old strategy, Putin is trying to create a new top position for himself or a political system that will provide security when he retires," Stanovaya said. "He wants to create some conditions and mechanisms not only to govern the future of the president, but also to help him at some point."
In a speech to the National Assembly on Wednesday, Putin proposed that the presidential term be limited to two terms and that he did not want to run for president in the third term because it was unconstitutional. He also revealed plans to transfer power from the president to the lower house, an intention seen as a coup d'état or abolition of power or the influence of his successors.
It should be noted that Vladimir Putin has not yet set a date for the election on the constitutional amendment. Russia last held a referendum in 1993, when Russia adopted the current constitution under former President Boris Yeltsin.
