Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin Meets Cambodian People in Canberra and Informs the Nation of the Nation
(Canberra, Australia): Taking part in the 28th APPF Meeting in Australia, Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly, met with Cambodian people and students living and studying in Cambodia. Canberra and nearby states on the afternoon of January 16, 2020 at the Cambodian Embassy in Canberra, Australia.
Mr. Chan Ratana, Cambodian Ambassador to Australia, said that the people and students who came to welcome Samdech expressed their deepest condolences to the President of the National Assembly. The people thanked Samdech for his struggle to liberate the country from the Khmer Rouge regime and for his contribution to the development of the country until today.
Cambodians living abroad express their support and happiness for the progress of peace that Cambodia has achieved today and ask the people's leaders in the country to work together to maintain lasting peace and development. They do not want to see the country at war, which leads to the division of the Khmer people and to flee to another country.
The President of the National Assembly thanked the Cambodian people living in Australia who have welcomed him since arriving at the airport. At the same time, Samdech thanked all Cambodians living abroad, especially Cambodians living in Australia, who have always supported the people in the country, both mentally and materially, to contribute to the building and development of the country from the beginning. .
Samdech instructed the Cambodian Ambassador to Australia that all people know how to help each other at all times, be tolerant and maintain the dignity of Cambodian citizens.
At the same time, Samdech Akka Moha Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin informed the Cambodian people overseas about the actual situation in the country, especially the current development of the nation. On that occasion, Samdech recalled the history of the war in Cambodia and the resistance movement of patriotic elites who liberated the country from the Khmer Rouge regime and the struggle to build the nation after January 7, 1979.
