Apsara Authority restores 52 km of ancient irrigation in Angkor and is ready to restore more
(Siem Reap): As of 2020, it has been 25 years since the APSARA Authority was established, in which many achievements, especially water management achievements in the Angkor resort, also occurred together, while the principle of continuing to restore the ancient irrigation system Others are also being put out and put into practice.
APSARA Authority spokesman Mr. Long Kosal said that until now, the national experts are more capable, so the APSARA Authority has enough human resources to support the conservation and restoration of the temple.
He said that water management, which is also an important task, has also achieved many successes by restoring a total of 52 km of ancient irrigation system.
Since 2004, the APSARA Authority has started to study the ancient structure of the irrigation system and has reached the stage of restoration. In fact, in 2008, the APSARA Authority restored the drainage system originating from Phnom Kulen into the Chey Tadak baray, or northern baray, after more than 500 years without water.
Mr. Long Kosal added that this point is very important that the APSARA Authority strives to restore the ancient irrigation system for the preservation of the temple, as well as for daily use and irrigation of agricultural products of the people.
APSARA Authority also restored the main shrines in the Angkor area, including: Preah Sin, Preah Khan, Angkor Wat, and Angkor Thom, which previously had only water during the rainy season.
Currently, both the baray and the temple can hold a maximum of more than 100 million cubic meters of water. Apart from being used for the preservation of the temple, APSARA Authority also distributes water from Teuk Thla baray to the people's rice fields on an area of 7,500 hectares, cultivating various crops of the people, as well as distributing to the Water Supply Authority to produce clean water. Supply daily consumption to the people of Siem Reap to another level.
According to Mr. Long Kosal, after the success of these water management, the National Authority also has a policy to restore other ancient irrigation structures that are now dry and run out of water for the purpose of preserving the ancient irrigation system and as a source of water for use. People today. Chinese Consulate General in Siem Reap donates medical equipment, computers and printers to 3 health centers worth more than 30,000 USD
