The proposal of the Japanese MP was made during a meeting with Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance, on the morning of January 16, 2020 at the Ministry of Economy.
In response to the request regarding the agreement to avoid double taxation with Japan, Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth urged the relevant technical working groups of the two countries to continue further negotiations to find a common solution for Go ahead and sign this agreement soon.
So far, Cambodia has signed agreements with nine countries and regions to avoid double taxation: the Republic of Singapore, Brunei Darussalam, the People's Republic of China, the Kingdom of Thailand, and the Socialist Republic of Vietnam. Republic of Indonesia, Hong Kong Special Administrative Region of the People's Republic of China, Malaysia and South Korea.
YAMAMOTO Kozo's visit to Cambodia aims to study, understand and exchange experiences with the Cambodian side on the development of the financial sector, especially on the use of new technologies in the banking sector.
During the meeting, Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth extended a warm welcome to Mr. YAMAMOTO Kozo, Member of the National Assembly of Japan, who spent his valuable time working in Japan. Visit to Cambodia.
The Deputy Prime Minister highlighted the great merits of the Government and people of Japan to the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia, from helping the peace process to development in all areas.
He said that the friendship and cooperation between the two countries, Cambodia and Japan have been constantly strengthened and expanded and have brought many fruitful achievements, especially in the framework of technical assistance, grants and concessional loans. At the same time, the inflow of direct investment from Japan has also increased, which has helped create jobs, transfer experience and knowledge, as well as contribute to reducing poverty in Cambodia.
He spoke about the history of Japan's economic construction, financial sector reforms and the promotion of non-paper payment methods through the use of technology.
In the financial sector, the Royal Government of Cambodia has a long-term policy framework on the development of the digital economy, which encourages the use of technology in the financial sector, both in the public and private sectors. The Royal Government of Cambodia is also implementing financial management information systems within the framework of the Public Financial Management Reform Program.
Meanwhile, the National Bank of Cambodia has developed the Bakong system, a digital payment system to enhance the security and efficiency of paperless payments.
Taking this opportunity, once again, Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth expressed his deep gratitude to the Government and people of Japan for their continued support to the Royal Government and the people of Cambodia and for their valuable contributions to Economic and social development in Cambodia.
