March 2018 - On March 8, 2018, US President Donald Trump announced an increase in tariffs to 25 percent on steel products and 10 percent on aluminum products imported from several countries with the aim of reducing deficits. America's largest trade. While Trump has given exemptions to some countries, China is not named.
August 2018 - Washington raises tariffs on $ 16 billion worth of Chinese goods, and Beijing retaliates by raising tariffs to 25 percent on US goods worth $ 16 billion.
September 2018: Washington continues to raise tariffs, this time by 10 percent on Chinese goods worth up to $ 200 million, and as before, China has issued a tariff increase in response.
December 2018 - Both sides declare war on tax increases, and the United States postpones its tariff plan for three months. China has promised to increase purchases of US agricultural products, suspend tariffs on vehicles and auto parts, and agree to open up rice imports from the United States.
* August 2019: On August 1, 2019, Mr. Tram announced a 10 percent tariff increase on other Chinese goods worth $ 300 billion, and later decided to increase it. At a rate of 15 percent. China has also retaliated by raising tariffs on many US goods.
September 2019: After the US tariff increase takes effect on September 1, 2019, China complains to the World Trade Organization. On September 4, 2019, Donald Trump announced an increase in tariffs on Chinese steel. Later, as negotiations resumed, Washington decided to postpone and cancel the tariff plan, which was due on October 15, 2019.
January 2020 - On January 13, 2020, Washington removes the Chinese name from "countries with erroneous monetary policy." In a joint statement issued by the US Treasury Department and the US Trade Representative on January 14, 2020, in the first phase of the trade agreement, there was no easing or cancellation of tariffs on Chinese goods. No.
