The habit of reading books began to become part of Cambodian culture
The event has been running since January 9, 2020 and will end on January 20, 2020, located at Borey Rong Roeung Market, Khan Chroy Changva, Phnom Penh. The largest book fair in the world, while attending the book fair is free.
This big event saw a lot of Cambodians come to visit and buy books in large numbers. At the exhibition venue, in an interview with Fresh News, people who went to buy books realized that reading habits were gradually becoming part of Cambodian culture.
Ms. Ly Suong's family is a family that loves to read books. She took her husband and children for a walk and allowed her children to choose the books of their choice. Three people, a husband and a daughter, Ms. Ly Suong's family bought about 50 books.
Ms. Ly Suong said that her family loves to read books, while they always spend time reading books every day. She also expressed her happiness that her daughter is also a reader and that she buys whatever books she buys.
Ms. Ly Suong hopes that reading books will definitely help her child's future. She said that her relatives also like to read books, because apparently at Ly Suong's relatives' house also has a book room. Ms. Ly Suong claims that whenever there are guests visiting relatives' houses, she always allows guests to bring books to read.
Saying the same thing, Mr. Yang Eikong, a second year student at the University of Technology of Cambodia, also sees that reading books has become a good habit for Cambodians. Obviously, students like Mr. Ikong always read new and good things to learn more about the whole world.
According to this student, most people like to read books when they are in university, because university is a time to learn life skills. He told other young people to work hard to read books and promote reading to become highly educated can help the country.
"As a student, I ask that they do not go the wrong way, must turn to study first, so that our country can grow into a bamboo vine." Mr. Yang Ikong emphasized.
In 2019, the exhibition was held in 32 cities in 10 countries in Southeast Asia, South Asia, the Middle East, which attracted more than 5 million participants. For Cambodia, this is the first time there has been such a big book exhibition.
Ms. Kim Muy, the information agent of the 1 million book fair, said that the reason for this event was because of the strong support of the Cambodian people for reading books, which was demonstrated through the book fair in National Library a few months ago. She revealed that "Big Bad Wolf Books" did not expect a lot of people to support because the exhibition is all English books, but obviously people came to support the expectations.
"At first I did not expect so many people, because this event is all about foreign language books, so I think there may be a few people who came, but suddenly more than expected," said Kim. . More and more from day to day, especially on weekends.
