(Tbong Khmum): More than 70 pig and cattle butchers in Suong City, Tbong Khmum Province, are protesting against tax officials in Tbong Khmum Province who continue to levy taxes on slaughter as the Royal Government abolishes the slaughter tax since July last year. As of 2017.
Heng Hong Seng, a representative of the Suong Pig and Cattle Slaughterhouse, said that during July 2017, they all received a letter from the Ministry of Economy and Finance stating that in all capitals, provinces and cities across the country, the tax should be stopped. Livestock businesses make traders across the country congratulate and support the government on exempting the slaughter tax.
However, tax officials continued to collect taxes until November 2018, when they all stopped paying taxes after realizing that across the country, there is no longer a tax on slaughter, only municipal tax officials. The Ministry of Foreign Affairs contrary to the decision of Samdech Techea Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia on July 8, 2017 to abolish the tax on slaughter.
Regarding the above case, the head of Tbong Khmum provincial tax branch, Mr. Chhim Veng Ngon, explained that he did not collect tax on slaughter, but he collected tax on business, that is, if the turnover is 250 million riels per year. This means that if the turnover is less than 250 million riels, it is not taxable. This tax collection is changed from a contractor regime to a self-declared regime.
However, according to Mr. Teng Chray, the owner of the Suong slaughterhouse tax collector, after Samdech Techo Hun Sen's note on the abolition of the slaughter tax, he questioned the same slaughterhouse owner in Phnom Penh. Phnom Penh, as in some provinces, the tax officials no longer collect taxes on slaughter, nor do they apply the self-declaration regime like in Suong.
Mr. Teng Chray said that as he had attended seminars related to the Royal Government, the decision to abolish the slaughter tax was intended to alleviate the people's difficulty in spending more money to buy meat for food. It is also a strategy to reduce people's poverty.
Even in Tbong Khmum province, some markets such as Orang Ov Market, Krek Market, Memot Market, etc. do not have a tax officer to collect slaughter tax or self-declared tax, only a group of butchers. Only Suong City encounters the above problems.
Mr. Heng Hong Seng, on behalf of 75 butchers, requested the Ministry of Economy and Finance to provide guidance and clarification regarding Samdech Techo Hun Sen's memorandum on "Abolition of Taxes". Slaughter »What does it mean? In order for the butchers to follow. On the other hand, why such tax collection is not available in other provinces and cities, and only in Suong.
