(Makassar): Osman Hassan, Senior Minister in charge of Special Missions and a member of the Supreme Council of the Muslim World Alliance RABITA, attended the 44th Supreme Council Meeting in Makkah, Saudi Arabia, with dignitaries from 82 countries.
RABITA, founded in 1962 and headquartered in Makkah, Saudi Arabia, was founded by King Faisal al-Saud by decision of the Organization of Islamic Cooperation (OIC), an influential Islamic organization. Funded by the Government of Saudi Arabia. This organization was established with the aim of expanding the relationship of Islamic and humanitarian cooperation worldwide.
The organization adheres to a moderate path and prevents extremist ideologies or ideas that Islam prohibits and defames the sacred religion, and promotes the cooperation of the Islamic State under the auspices of the OIC. Fill in the gaps of this organization and jointly strengthen relations between all Muslim countries in the world.
The Supreme Council is the supreme authority of the World Islamic Alliance, which approves all plans submitted by the 60-member General Secretariat, which is a senior Islamic elite representing the nations and communities of each country.
The Islamic Organization of the World (ILO) has several major associations covering 95 countries, calling on the Muslim nation to adhere to its brotherly unity in accordance with Islamic law and to avoid divisions. Indeed, the Muslim nation that is experiencing divisions, as it is today, is the result of arbitrary practices.
In addition to the Supreme Council meeting, Senior Minister Osman Hassan also met with representatives of Islamic leaders.
