The operation was carried out on the orders of General Dr. Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner General, and at the request of a Royal Government delegation in charge of the General Fisheries Administration of the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries.
As a result of the investigation, the Anti-Technology Crime Department arrested a 31-year-old suspect, Nguyen Yang Thev, in front of a rented house on National Road No. 1, Sdao Kanleng Village, Dei Edth Commune, Kien Svay District, Kandal Province, and confiscated a fishing tackle. 2 recycled units attached.
After that, the force of the Department, in cooperation with the Kandal Provincial Commissioner and the local authorities, and with the coordination led by Mr. Tin Sochetra, Deputy Prosecutor of the Kandal Provincial Prosecutor's Office, inspected the suspects' houses and found 9 processed fishing tackle devices. And bring the evidence with the suspect to the specialized department for processing.
Seeing this, the Royal Government delegation in charge of the General Administration of Fisheries requested the specialized officers of the Anti-Technology Crime Department of the General Commission of the National Police to help search and find the suspects and punish them because the use of electric fishing tackle is a criminal offense. Stated in the Fisheries Law. On the other hand, the use of electric gear for fishing is the most serious damage to fish resources.
According to the suspect's confession, Nguyen Yang Thev said that he first repaired the engine, fan, cooker and locksmith, then went to learn how to make a fishing tackle because it is so popular.
It then started to produce high-efficiency fishing tackle and promote it, which has been sold to many customers in Kampong Cham, Ratanakiri, Pursat, Takeo, etc. By asking customers to deposit half the money, then when it is finished, they will send the equipment by taxi to their customers.
A letter from the Council of Ministers states: "The Royal Government has agreed in principle to relocate to a new location for the airport on an area of approximately 400 to 600 hectares in Koh Por village, Pak Klang commune, Mondul Seima district, Koh Kong province." "The Secretariat of State for Civil Aviation must continue the procedure to begin construction."
Mr. Sin Chanserivutha, Deputy Director General of Technology and Spokesperson for Civil Aviation, confirmed to Fresh News on 13 January 2020 that the Secretariat of State for Civil Aviation supports the relocation of the old location to the new location. Skills need to be studied to ensure that Cambodian flights do not affect Thai airspace.
