Lessons from the case of building collapse
(Beijing, China): First of all, I would like to express my deepest condolences to the victims and their families in the case of the collapse of buildings in Sihanoukville, Siem Reap and Kep. May the souls of the dead be healed, while the wounded be healed.
At the same time, I would like to express my sincere respect and admiration for Samdech Techo Hun Sen's great spirit and love for all Cambodians. Samdech Techo always considers the well-being of the people as a priority at all times, not only when the people are suffering. This is the luck of the Cambodian people.
In fact, in the last half year alone, there have been at least three cases of building collapse. On June 23, 2019, a seven-storey building collapsed in Sihanoukville, killing 28 people and injuring 26 others. On December 2, 2019, a temple collapsed in Siem Reap, killing three people and injuring 13 others.
One month later, on January 3, 2020, another six-story building collapsed in Kep province, killing 36 workers and children and injuring 23 others.
What can Cambodia learn from the collapse of these buildings? There are many things that Cambodia may have to learn from these issues. At least there are some points that the general public can see:
First, the implementation of construction laws and regulations is still limited: the high rate of projects and constructions that have started construction without a permit clearly shows this, especially in the case of Sihanoukville. In addition, there may be shortages of technical inspections, plans and components, impact assessments, soil quality assessments and inspections. Therefore, law enforcement should be a priority.
Second: Corruption in the construction sector: The case of the director of the department and his party in the Cadastral Department in Pursat province is a good lesson. The relevant ministries should increase the inspection and scrubbing of such officials. Informal running services should also be minimized.
Third: Occupational safety in the construction sector is low: safety system, pre-entry and exit inspections and construction workers' knowledge on safety is still limited. States and companies should tighten and train basic knowledge on workplace safety.
Before entering the construction site, all workers must wear safety equipment such as helmets and gloves. Long-term construction should provide accommodation to workers outside of the construction site under construction. In particular, construction workers should receive social protection, at least short-term insurance.
Fifth, some officials in charge and related still cause difficulties to the country's leaders: If you love the leaders and the people, please participate in their work with high responsibility. The irresponsible actions of some officials not only tired the leaders, but also demoralized the people.
(Yen Yat, Postdoctoral Researcher at Peking University)
