As in previous years, about 30,000 Cambodians in Phnom Penh on the morning of January 7, 2020 gathered to celebrate January 7 Victory Day, which they consider their second birthday. The solemn ceremony was presided over by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and President of the Cambodian People's Party, and Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly and Honorary President of the Cambodian People's Party.
In addition to gatherings in Phnom Penh, the provinces also celebrated the fall of Democratic Kampuchea. In Phnom Penh, the people who attended the ceremony showed fresh faces with bright smiles as a manifestation of their joy for the day to help them live again.
The Democratic Kampuchea regime of 3 years, 8 months and 20 days was a regime that tortured, suppressed freedoms and brutally massacred. Nearly 2 million Cambodians, young and old, died during the regime as a result of torture, starvation, disease, and genocide.
The suffering of the people in this genocidal regime, even though it has been gone for about 41 years. But this tragedy and excruciating pain remain in the memory of the people who went through this regime.
Pov Samoch is also a Cambodian victim of the Democratic Kampuchea regime. Under that regime, she was taken to be a sugarcane grower in Battambang province. Pov Samoch, now 71, likened life to the Pol Pot genocidal regime to life in hell.
She further described that life in Democratic Kampuchea was lived in fear and unknowingly, when was she killed? In addition to remembering the brutal act of slaughter, living a life of hunger for three cans of rice, a bag of porridge mixed with banana roots is also a memory of Ms. Pov Samoch.
What Pov Samoch remembers is the separation of the couple and their children, and even sick family members are not allowed to visit.
The story that Pov Samoch will never forget is that the regime killed four of her siblings, including three brothers and one younger brother, for allegedly betraying the organization.
Another victim of the Pol Pot genocidal regime, Ms. Bun Sim, still remembers the hardships that befell her and her family during that regime. Ms. Bun Sim is now 62 years old. During the Khmer Rouge regime, she and her family were evacuated from Phnom Penh to Battambang province.
Ms. Bun Sim suffered the same torture as other people. Unable to endure the tragic torture, Ms. Bun Sim even considered suicide. But because she has small children, she has to endure all the hardships in this regime.
The women who swam through the two genocidal regimes never imagined that this genocidal regime had been overthrown and always prayed to the gods for someone to fight to overthrow this genocidal regime.
Ms. Pov Samoch and Ms. Bun Sim expressed their gratitude for the liberation on January 7. The two survivors of the Khmer Rouge claimed that without the January 7 liberation, they would not have survived.
Victory on January 7, because many Cambodians consider it a day to liberate their lives from death and misery. However, some people have tried to say that the January 7 victory was an event that paved the way for the Vietnamese army to invade Cambodia.
Ms. Pov Samoch did not agree with this statement. Ms. Pov Samoch saw that the presence of the Vietnamese army was through Khmer fighters, and without the presence of the Vietnamese army, it would not have been possible to overthrow the genocidal regime.
Cambodia has transformed from a killing field of genocidal regimes, from a battlefield of chronic war, from a land of no freedom, from national division and division, and from a land of ashes to a land of peace, stability, freedom, democracy, the rule of law, and strong development. All spheres make the people live in peace and hope for their future under the roof of the Constitution and the cool shadow of the King, the highest shrine.
Ms. Pov Samoch and Ms. Bun Sim saw that Cambodia has developed greatly in time to be liberated from the genocidal regime that has nothing. Both Ms. Pov Samoch and Ms. Bun Sim said that after the liberation, the people were still hungry and had to eat bananas, papaya.
Unlike 41 years ago, Cambodia today has greatly improved the country's infrastructure, the lives of civilized people. This is the confirmation of Ms. Pov Samoch and Ms. Bun Sim.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia and President of the Cambodian People's Party (CPP), said that although Cambodia has overcome all difficulties to bring about the glorious national progress as it is now, the road ahead will still have obstacles. More. But no obstacle can stop the journey of the Cambodian nation as long as we maintain national unity and continue to travel on the right path forward.
In celebrating the Victory Day on January 7, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also called on the people to unite to protect the peace that has been achieved with the utmost difficulty, to be strong and stable, and not to allow any reactionary forces to destroy it. No, no matter what the cost.
Samdech Techo emphasized to the Cambodian people that peace only guarantees sustainable development and the peaceful and prosperous life of the people, peace guarantees the stability and growth of democracy and freedom, and the success of the development process and The strength of democracy and, in turn, ensures the sustainability of peace. If we lose peace, we will lose everything.
